//? Title: Detection by patch classification
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Object detection must locate and recognize every object in an image, rather than classify the whole frame. This report fits an eleven-way tensor patch CNN and compares sliding windows with connected-component proposals on real MNIST digits composited into scenes. It shows pixel-centre crops, actual training data, the full Adam loop, held-out AP and annotated predictions.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.6_object_detection/detection_baselines.cpp`.

> The method

A detector proposes boxes, resamples fixed-size patches, classifies them and suppresses duplicates. Exhaustive square windows offer predictable coverage but can fit narrow digit boxes poorly. Component proposals use the blank background to suggest tighter regions, while touching strokes can merge objects. Both methods below share the same learned classifier; only proposal geometry changes.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/detection_baselines_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/detection_baselines_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/detection_baselines_concept.tikz > build/detection_baselines_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/detection_baselines_concept.svg build/detection_baselines_concept.dvi > build/detection_baselines_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
