\caption(The same selected patch classifier on sliding windows and component proposals. Candidate coverage is measured before classification. Connected points compare two methods; they do not represent a continuous parameter sweep.)
\alttext(Two panels compare held-out AP and candidate recall for sliding windows and connected-component proposals.)


> Conclusion

The worked crop verifies pixel-centre geometry, and the data galleries show the actual scenes and sampled classifier patches. Both proposal methods then use the same validation-selected tensor CNN, score threshold and per-image/class NMS. Candidate recall and held-out AP separate localization coverage from learned classification. On 23 held-out digits, component proposals use 11.75 candidates per image
versus 454 windows. Their proposal recall at IoU 0.5/0.7 is 0.869565/0.695652 versus
0.782609/0.391304, and inclusive VOC AP50 is 0.674392 versus 0.421627. COCO-style
AP75 is 0.345508 versus 0.037404. The gallery retains missed, merged and extra boxes
visibly. These eight scenes support a bounded mechanism comparison, not the
full source run or a real detection benchmark.

> References

- [LeCun et al. (1998), Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
- [Girshick et al. (2014), Rich feature hierarchies for accurate object detection and semantic segmentation](https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.2524).
- [PASCAL VOC2007 evaluation conventions](https://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/~vgg/projects/pascal/VOC/voc2007/index.html).
- [COCO official evaluator](https://github.com/cocodataset/cocoapi/blob/master/PythonAPI/pycocotools/cocoeval.py).
